# SENTINEL AI: build prompt 1 (complete security-company site, EN / MN)

You are building **SENTINEL AI**, the website of a security company in Ulaanbaatar that sells AI surveillance, smart access control and zero-trust networks. It has a full set of pages, a small database for what visitors send in, and three source designs merged into one site:

- **Appendix A: "SENTINEL AI" Spline hero.** The home page hero, the fixed navbar, the colour tokens, the font and the two animations.
- **Appendix B: Facet "Designed in glass" WebGL hero.** A second full-screen section on the home page, re-themed as "Security in glass".
- **Appendix C: EngineTech footer.** The site footer on every page.

This prompt has two parts:

1. **The master layer** (this part): what the site is, how pages connect, what changes from the three source designs, the data, the languages, and how Mongolian must behave.
2. **Three source designs** (Appendices A, B, C), pasted verbatim. They define every pixel, timing, easing, colour, 3D value and interaction of the hero, the glass section and the footer.

## Decisions at a glance

| Question | Decision |
|---|---|
| Where does the Facet glass hero go? | Home page, **second full-screen section**, directly under the Spline hero (`#glass`). It is not a separate page. |
| What is it about? | "Security in glass": the glass shards carry the company's principles. The "Read paper" shatter reveals **five security terms** (zero trust, edge inference, access control, audit log, response time). |
| Where is the EN / MN toggle? | **Desktop (≥1280px):** in the fixed top bar, immediately **left of the "Get Quote" button**. **Below 1280px:** in the top bar, **left of the menu button**. It also appears in the footer's legal line. |
| Palette | Two colours: charcoal (`#141414`, `#1A1A1A`, card `#292929`, footer black `#000000`, glass `#050507`) and one vivid green `#05E901`. Red appears only for form errors. No third accent. |
| Fonts | **Sora** (Latin, weights 300 to 700) with **Inter** as the Cyrillic fallback and as the font of the glass section. |
| Default language | Mongolian. English on request. |
| 3D products | Three: security camera, access reader, network hub. One GLB slot each in `public/models/`, plus built-in procedural models, so the page never breaks if a file is missing. |
| Special 404 | "Outside the perimeter." with a radar scan, a real 404 status code and quick links. |
| Backend | Express + plain PostgreSQL (`pg`) in production, `node:sqlite` locally. Two tables: `contact_messages`, `quote_requests`. No Firebase, no Supabase client SDK. |

## How to read the appendices

- The appendices are law for **layout, sizes, timing, easing, colours, the 3D scene, interactions and responsive behaviour** of the Spline hero, the glass section and the footer. Do not "improve" them. Do not change an easing, a duration, a size or a breakpoint.
- This master layer wins only where it says so, and only for: brand, copy, data, language, routes, navigation, fonts for Cyrillic, how the code is organised, and the places listed under "Overrides" in the sections below. Every override has a reason (contrast, Cyrillic width, robustness, keyboard use). Apply them exactly; do not add others.
- Ignore the sentences in the appendices about "plain HTML, CSS and JS", "a single standalone file", "no build step", "an import map" and "three.js from jsDelivr". Here the site is a multi-route React app (see "Stack"). three.js comes from npm, pinned to `0.169.0`, the version Appendix B names.
- Every visible word comes from the i18n files (see "Language switching"), including the strings the appendices write in English.
- Real brands and logos never appear. Facet and EngineTech are source-design names only; the words "Facet", "EngineTech", "Columbus, OH" and every sentence about propulsion or material studios must not appear on the site.

## Stack and code quality

- **React 19 + Vite + TypeScript** (`.tsx` / `.ts`), **Tailwind CSS 4** (`@tailwindcss/vite`, tokens in `@theme`), **shadcn/ui** components (Button, Input, Textarea, Label, Checkbox, Sheet), `class-variance-authority`, `clsx`, `tailwind-merge`, `tw-animate-css`, `lucide-react` (icons: Menu, X, ArrowRight, ArrowUpRight, Check), `react-router-dom` 7.
- **3D:** `@splinetool/react-spline` and `@splinetool/runtime` for the home hero (exact versions from the latest stable at build time, pinned without `^`). `three` pinned to exactly `"0.169.0"` and `@types/three` to `"0.169.0"` for the glass section and the product viewer. Import `RoomEnvironment`, `GLTFLoader`, `DRACOLoader`, `OrbitControls` and `RoundedBoxGeometry` from `three/examples/jsm/...`, and the Meshopt decoder from `three/examples/jsm/libs/meshopt_decoder.module.js`. The Draco decoder path is `https://www.gstatic.com/draco/versioned/decoders/1.5.7/`. The three.js code is loaded with a dynamic `import()` only when its section is near the viewport, so the first page load carries no three.js.
- **Server:** Express 4 in `server.ts` (run by Node 22.18 or newer, which strips types itself: no TypeScript-only runtime syntax such as enums or parameter properties). `node:sqlite` locally, `pg` through `DATABASE_URL` in production, `zod`, `helmet`, `express-rate-limit`. Dev runs through `tsx`. No Firebase or other backend-as-a-service (see "Database").
- `package.json`: `"engines": { "node": ">=22.18" }`, a `.node-version` file with `22`, scripts `dev` (`tsx server.ts`), `build` (`vite build`), `start` (`node server.ts`), `typecheck` (`tsc --noEmit`), `check:i18n`, `db:check`, `test` (`node --test`). **Do not add `esbuild` as a dependency** (it conflicts with Vite's optional peer and breaks `npm install` with ERESOLVE). `npm install` must succeed on a clean clone with no flags, and the repository commits `package-lock.json` and no other lock file. `npm run typecheck` must report zero errors. `build` does not run the type check, so a type nit can never block a deploy.
- The code must read like it was written by a careful human engineer who will hand it to a full-stack team: clear camelCase names, one responsibility per file, short comments only where the reason is not obvious, no leftover prompt wording, no dead code, no `console.log` noise, no `any` (use `unknown` and narrow), no `// @ts-ignore`.
- No secrets in the client. Every setting comes from environment variables and is listed in `.env.example`.

